    tests_nosleep A10 host-independent (arm64 migration 2026-09-27): the red capability is manufactured, never borrowed from the host's log size
    
    Old A10 ran the REAL `pmset -g log` under a 0.5 s timeout and relied on the old Intel host's 185 MB store taking 74 s to render. The arm64 host
    renders its store in 0.1 s, so A10 read ([], 0.1) and went red with nothing wrong in the code; every battery on the new host is 165/166 and
    safe_commit (all-green required) refuses every release. Test-only change; ops/check_nosleep.py untouched.
    
    - _host_sealed(serve): inside, CN._run(cmd) runs serve(cmd) through the REAL _run with the caller's timeout (real subprocess, real
      TimeoutExpired); a command serve() does not answer is refused (rc 126) and recorded — nothing inside a seal reaches this machine.
    - A10p positive control: a fast synthetic reader printing one fixture pmset line => a LIST with that sleep (so None below is the timeout).
    - A10 red capability: a synthetic child that sleeps 10 s under timeout 0.5 => None, returned in < 5 s.
    - A10h: both calls were `pmset -g log` and both were served; zero host calls inside a seal.
    - A10s (class): every check titled RED CAPABILITY / RED CONTROL in this file sits inside a `with _host_sealed(...)` — derived from the
      file's own AST, at least one seen (never 0/0); guard control on a planted source names both unsealed labels and passes the sealed one.
    - A9 (THIS MACHINE positive, real store) unchanged.
    
    Measured (arm64, /usr/bin/python3 3.9.6): suite ALL PASS; mutation ladder 7/7 as expected (baseline green; timeout dropped,
    failure-reads-as-empty, _run swallowing the timeout => A10 red; reader command changed => A10p + A10h red; parser drops the sleep => A10p
    red; red check moved out of its seal => A10s red). Census: only tests_nosleep (A9 intended, A10) and tests_entrypoint_wiring (synthetic log)
    touch host power/log commands in the executor battery.
    
